WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.858 --> 00:00:06.129
A medium-voltage ring runs from the substation past seven secondary substations,

2
00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:09.345
with 1,200 customers between them.

3
00:00:10.088 --> 00:00:13.885
Station 7 holds the normally open point to a neighbouring ring:

4
00:00:14.059 --> 00:00:16.845
the switch in an MV ring that is kept open;

5
00:00:17.043 --> 00:00:19.992
you close it to feed a section from the other side.

6
00:00:21.058 --> 00:00:24.854
Then a digger hits the cable between stations 5 and 6.

7
00:00:25.594 --> 00:00:28.241
The protection relay sees the short circuit,

8
00:00:28.427 --> 00:00:31.330
and the circuit breaker in the substation trips.

9
00:00:32.036 --> 00:00:33.139
Everyone is off.

10
00:00:34.133 --> 00:00:38.138
Without automation, a technician drives from station to station.

11
00:00:38.834 --> 00:00:40.309
He reads the indicators,

12
00:00:40.529 --> 00:00:44.407
opens the load-break switches around the damaged section by hand,

13
00:00:44.581 --> 00:00:46.381
and closes the open point.

14
00:00:47.126 --> 00:00:50.725
That means one to two hours without power for most customers.

15
00:00:51.715 --> 00:00:54.153
Say all of them are off for 90 minutes.

16
00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:57.683
The grid operator counts that in customer minutes:

17
00:00:57.823 --> 00:01:01.073
the number of customers off times the minutes without power.

18
00:01:01.843 --> 00:01:07.323
1,200 times 90 is 108,000 customer minutes.

19
00:01:08.608 --> 00:01:11.673
Distribution automation means remote control

20
00:01:11.743 --> 00:01:14.088
and monitoring of the medium-voltage grid.

21
00:01:15.105 --> 00:01:17.601
Some stations get a remote-controlled switch:

22
00:01:17.729 --> 00:01:20.480
a motor-driven load-break switch that the control

23
00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:24.184
room can open and close over a communication link.

24
00:01:25.202 --> 00:01:29.927
In the station you see a small cabinet with a modem, a battery and the controls,

25
00:01:30.113 --> 00:01:31.982
and the motor drive on the switch.

26
00:01:32.699 --> 00:01:36.832
The battery keeps everything working when the station has lost its supply,

27
00:01:36.960 --> 00:01:39.270
which is exactly during an outage.

28
00:01:40.290 --> 00:01:44.446
In the control room, the operator sees in SCADA where the fault is,

29
00:01:44.574 --> 00:01:48.486
and switches in minutes, without waiting for a technician to arrive.

30
00:01:49.811 --> 00:01:54.501
Each ring panel has a fault indicator: a sensor round the cable and a lamp.

31
00:01:55.253 --> 00:01:59.143
It lights up, or sends a signal, when fault current has passed it.

32
00:02:00.188 --> 00:02:03.788
Fault current flows from the source to the fault, and no further.

33
00:02:04.509 --> 00:02:10.175
Here the indicators in stations 1 to 5 light up; in 6 and 7 they stay dark.

34
00:02:11.247 --> 00:02:16.204
So the fault lies between the last indicator that saw it and the first that did not:

35
00:02:16.344 --> 00:02:18.666
between stations 5 and 6.

36
00:02:19.698 --> 00:02:23.878
Indicators that report to the control room save the drive to read them.

37
00:02:24.591 --> 00:02:28.399
Even without remote switching, that alone can save many minutes.

38
00:02:29.115 --> 00:02:34.328
How the control room handles the whole outage is in Grid operations and assets.

39
00:02:35.673 --> 00:02:41.152
Now the same fault, with remote-controlled switches in two key stations: station 4,

40
00:02:41.292 --> 00:02:43.637
and station 7 with the open point.

41
00:02:44.686 --> 00:02:48.796
The control room opens the switch towards 5 in station 4,

42
00:02:48.982 --> 00:02:51.432
and the one towards 6 in station 7.

43
00:02:52.163 --> 00:02:55.321
The breaker goes back in and the open point closes.

44
00:02:56.036 --> 00:03:01.528
The faulty section is isolated in 3 minutes, and 1,000 customers are back.

45
00:03:02.564 --> 00:03:07.359
The 200 customers of stations 5 and 6 are in the section between.

46
00:03:08.071 --> 00:03:12.657
They stay off for 90 minutes, until the technician has switched there by hand.

47
00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:22.184
1,000 times 3 plus 200 times 90 is 3,000 plus 18,000: 21,000 customer minutes.

48
00:03:22.928 --> 00:03:29.150
That is about 80% less than 108,000, from just two upgraded stations.

49
00:03:30.450 --> 00:03:32.354
First, protection does its job:

50
00:03:32.505 --> 00:03:36.127
the circuit breaker in the substation clears the short circuit.

51
00:03:36.865 --> 00:03:40.418
A load-break switch never breaks a short circuit itself.

52
00:03:41.429 --> 00:03:45.226
A self-healing ring goes one step further than remote switching.

53
00:03:45.901 --> 00:03:48.722
The system locates the fault from the indicators,

54
00:03:48.897 --> 00:03:53.204
opens the switches around it and closes the open point by itself,

55
00:03:53.390 --> 00:03:54.516
often within a minute.

56
00:03:55.561 --> 00:04:00.507
It needs reliable communication, accurate network data and well-tested logic,

57
00:04:00.565 --> 00:04:03.468
so operators introduce it step by step.

58
00:04:04.542 --> 00:04:06.423
Experts argue about trust.

59
00:04:07.171 --> 00:04:12.605
Some control rooms want an operator to confirm each step; others let the logic run.

60
00:04:13.929 --> 00:04:16.901
Automating every station is too expensive.

61
00:04:17.610 --> 00:04:21.836
Operators choose a few key stations per ring, often two or three,

62
00:04:21.999 --> 00:04:25.598
where a remote-controlled switch splits the ring into sections.

63
00:04:26.616 --> 00:04:31.202
That is a business case: the cost per station against fewer customer minutes,

64
00:04:31.353 --> 00:04:32.363
and less driving.

65
00:04:33.401 --> 00:04:36.095
Unplanned outages leave Dutch customers without

66
00:04:36.187 --> 00:04:40.251
power for around 20 to 25 minutes a year on average,

67
00:04:40.402 --> 00:04:43.630
about 23 in 2025.

68
00:04:44.348 --> 00:04:48.260
And the regulator links part of the operators’ income to that quality.

69
00:04:49.546 --> 00:04:50.382
What goes wrong?

70
00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:55.031
Batteries that are flat when needed: then no motor and no modem.

71
00:04:56.023 --> 00:04:58.449
Communication that drops during a storm.

72
00:04:59.166 --> 00:05:03.972
And network data that do not match reality, so the system picks the wrong switch.

73
00:05:05.002 --> 00:05:09.274
And every link to a station is also a possible way in for attackers.

74
00:05:09.988 --> 00:05:14.574
So treat it as OT, with zones and conduits, just as in a plant.

75
00:05:15.677 --> 00:05:19.299
A sharp question: which rings cause the most customer minutes,

76
00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:21.877
and where would one remote switch help most?
